 𝗗𝗮𝘆 𝟮𝟱/𝟯𝟲𝟱 — 𝗗𝗲𝘃𝗢𝗽𝘀 𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗖𝗵𝗮𝗹𝗹𝗲𝗻𝗴𝗲

🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗶𝗻 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗦𝘁𝗮𝘁𝗲, 𝗕𝘂𝘁 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝘁𝗮𝗶𝗻𝗲𝗿 𝗖𝗣𝗨 𝗶𝘀 𝗛𝗶𝗴𝗵 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗗𝗼?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes application is running, but one pod is suddenly consuming very high CPU. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I wouldn't immediately restart the pod.

First, I would identify what is causing the CPU spike and whether it is affecting the application.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check pod CPU usage

kubectl top pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Compare the current usage with the pod's normal baseline.

2️⃣ Check the pod configuration

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Review the CPU requests and limits.

3️⃣ Check application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Look for:

🔹 Error loops
🔹 Excessive requests
🔹 Repeated retries
🔹 Background jobs
🔹 Recent application changes

4️⃣ Check whether traffic increased

I would compare current traffic with the normal baseline.

A sudden increase in requests can naturally cause higher CPU consumption.

5️⃣ Check recent deployments

I would investigate:

🔹 New application version
🔹 Configuration changes
🔹 New features
🔹 Dependency changes

6️⃣ Check the node

kubectl top node <node-name>

I would determine whether the issue is isolated to one pod or whether the entire node is under CPU pressure.

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

CPU Spike → Pod Metrics → Limits → Logs → Traffic → Recent Changes → Node Health → Root Cause

If the application is impacted, I would follow the approved production procedure for scaling or recovery.

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

High CPU is a symptom — not the root cause.

Before increasing CPU limits or restarting the pod, understand why CPU usage increased. 🚀
